$f(x) = |\ln x| - |x - 1| = \begin{cases} \ln$ x - (x - 1) & x $\ge$ 1 \\ -$\ln$ x + (x - 1) & 0 < x < 1 \end{cases}
 = \begin{cases} \ln x - x + 1 & x \ge 1 \\ -\ln x + x - 1 & 0 < x < 1 \end{cases}
f'(x) = \begin{cases} \frac{1}{x} - 1 & x \ge 1 \\ -\frac{1}{x} + 1 & 0 < x < 1 \end{cases}
$f'(1^+) = f'(1^-) = 0 \Rightarrow f(x)$ is differentiable $\forall x > 0 f'(x) < 0 \quad \forall x > 1 f'(x) < 0 $\quad \forall$ 0 < x < 1$
 \Rightarrow$ f(x)$ is decreasing  \forall$ x $\in$ (0, \infty)$
